bodyMd:
A solid is made of identical unit cubes standing on a floor grid of 3 rows and 4 columns. Row 1 is at the front and Row 3 at the back; Column 1 is on the left and Column 4 on the right, as seen from the front. Every stack rests on the floor with its cubes directly on top of one another.

The solid is described only by three orthographic views:

- the **top view** (plan), shown in the grid cells: "occupied" means at least one cube stands on that square, "empty" means none does;
- the **front view**, in the bottom row: the height, in cubes, of the view's strip above each column, which is the height of the tallest stack in that column;
- the **side view**, in the last column: the height, in cubes, of the view's strip for each row, which is the height of the tallest stack in that row.
